Yeah, since the OP is on a Unix work-alike, better
to have the selection script pass a clean copy of a
tuned defaults file through an embedded sed() script
to produce one output defaults file.

It's really easy, with the default file "OPTIONS"
setup, to re-write the input defaults script with
each option to be changed on a line alone to make
sed() parsing trivial and sed() actions
straightforward.

This is pretty much a classical example of where a
stream editor is the elegant answer to saving the
space that all those unchanged lines in the multiple
copies of the defaults files take.

Since "MS Windows" versions of sed() are available,
the same idea should work there.

Of coures, if you have access to the Korn shell,
with it's slick built-in menu writing system; life
is a pure diet of champaign for doing stuff like
this.

